\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Fine access consistently improves local forecasts across backgrounds. Points show paired local MAE differences one hour ahead relative to random acquisition M2. Each dot averages magnitudes and families within one background, and black bars indicate means. Lower is better. B contains eight backgrounds across four weeks. C reuses four backgrounds with fresh seeds and different conditions. M4 receives the larger full access budget. Differences between policies at the restricted budget are small and heterogeneous. The panels describe separate evaluations, with descriptive spread rather than confidence intervals based on independent households.}}{10}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:paired}{{3}{10}{Fine access consistently improves local forecasts across backgrounds. Points show paired local MAE differences one hour ahead relative to random acquisition M2. Each dot averages magnitudes and families within one background, and black bars indicate means. Lower is better. B contains eight backgrounds across four weeks. C reuses four backgrounds with fresh seeds and different conditions. M4 receives the larger full access budget. Differences between policies at the restricted budget are small and heterogeneous. The panels describe separate evaluations, with descriptive spread rather than confidence intervals based on independent households}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Locating the gap between changed observations and unchanged acquisition in panel B. (a) Counts cover 96 demand episodes. Rankings are compared during disturbances, while score comparisons cover the replay. The conditions are distinct and do not form a strict funnel. (b) Score changes are compared with the background's distance to the acquisition threshold. All 4,608 paired updates satisfy the sufficient condition for unchanged gate decisions, including zero changes omitted from the scatter. Meter identities and timestamps independently verify that request sequences remain unchanged.}}{11}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:path}{{4}{11}{Locating the gap between changed observations and unchanged acquisition in panel B. (a) Counts cover 96 demand episodes. Rankings are compared during disturbances, while score comparisons cover the replay. The conditions are distinct and do not form a strict funnel. (b) Score changes are compared with the background's distance to the acquisition threshold. All 4,608 paired updates satisfy the sufficient condition for unchanged gate decisions, including zero changes omitted from the scatter. Meter identities and timestamps independently verify that request sequences remain unchanged}{figure.4}{}}
